The terms "isototopologues' and "isotopomers" are used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitution. Here’s the difference:  
1. **Isototopologues**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) (cyclohexane) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) (hexaduterated cyclohexane) are isototopologues because they have the same molecular structure but differ in the presence of deuterium (\( ^2\text{H} \)) instead of hydrogen (\( ^1\text{H} \)).  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms but also in their molecular structure. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) (cyclohexane) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) (hexaduterated cyclohexane) are isotopomers because they differ in the position of deuterium atoms, leading to different molecular structures.  

Thus, the key difference is that isototopologues have the same molecular structure, while isotopomers may have different molecular structures due to isotopic substitution.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isototopologues: Same structure, different isotopic composition.} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Different structure, different isotopic composition.}
\end{aligned}
}

